"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes \c{authors}, a tool for generating the \c{AUTHORS}
files of git repositories from the identity register that \c{mailmap} uses.
For the command line interface refer to the \l{authors(1)} man page.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

The \c{authors} tool generates the \c{AUTHORS} file of a git repository.
This file lists everyone who contributed to the repository, each person
once and by their preferred name, in alphabetical order (so that the list
doesn't read as a ranking). It is what the copyright notice at the
beginning of every source file refers to when it names \"the IW4x
authors\" as the holder.

The contributors are the people behind the identities that the history of
the repository carries. Since the same person can appear in the history
under several identities, \c{authors} attributes each identity to its
person through the identity register, the same register that \c{mailmap}
uses to generate the \c{.mailmap} file. The register also records how each
person wants to be listed (see \l{#listing Listing People} for details).

Note also that \c{AUTHORS} only grows: once it lists a person, \c{authors}
keeps them listed whether or not the history carries them. This matters for
a repository that is restarted (built anew with only the audited parts of
an old repository), whose history starts from scratch even though the work
of its earlier contributors comes over with the imported files.

We begin with a walkthrough that generates the \c{AUTHORS} of a repository
and keeps it up to date, and then look at how people are listed and at what
\c{authors} does underneath in more detail.

\h1#start|Getting Started|

Let's start with the repository from the \c{mailmap} walkthrough, whose
history has two identities for Jane, and the register that attributes both
of them to her:

\
$ cd hello/
$ git log --format='%an <%ae>'
J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
jane <jane@example.org>

$ cat ../identities
: 1
name: Jane Doe
email: jane@example.org
alias: J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
\

The repository has no \c{AUTHORS} yet, so let's generate one:

\
$ authors ../identities

repository:         /src/hello
history identities: 2
people listed:      1

+This file lists the IW4x authors, ...
...
+Jane Doe

update '/src/hello/AUTHORS'? [y/n]: y
\

The first lines tell us that the history carries two identities and that
\c{AUTHORS} lists one person, since both identities are Jane's. Next comes
the new file in the \c{diff(1)} notation. It starts with a preamble that
says who the IW4x authors are (we have shortened it here) and ends with the
entries, of which there is one: Jane, listed by her preferred name.

Once we answer \c{y}, \c{authors} installs \c{AUTHORS} and commits it, the
same way \c{mailmap} commits the \c{.mailmap}.

Now let's say John contributes to the repository and would like his email
to appear next to his name. And let's say a bot that keeps the dependencies
up to date commits as well. We record both of them in the register:

\
: 1
name: Jane Doe
email: jane@example.org
alias: J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
:
name: John Roe
email: john@example.org
authors: email
:
name: Build Bot
email: bot@example.org
authors: none
\

The \c{authors} value says how the person is listed. John is listed with his
email and the bot is not listed at all. Jane has no \c{authors} value and
is listed by her name. Running \c{authors} again adds John:

\
$ authors ../identities

repository:         /src/hello
history identities: 5
people listed:      2

--- a/AUTHORS
+++ b/AUTHORS
@@ -7,3 +7,4 @@
 below once, by their preferred name, in alphabetical order:

 Jane Doe
+John Roe <john@example.org>

update '/src/hello/AUTHORS'? [y/n]: y
\

Notice that the history now carries five identities. Besides John and the
bot there is Jane's own commit of \c{AUTHORS}, made as Jane Doe. The commit
message lists the entries the update adds and removes:

\
$ git log -1 --format=%B
Update AUTHORS

  add    John Roe <john@example.org>
\

If we run \c{authors} again, it tells us that \c{AUTHORS} is up to date and
exits without changing anything. And the same as with \c{mailmap}, an
identity the register does not account for makes \c{authors} list it and
fail:

\
$ authors ../identities
error: history of '/src/hello' has identities not in '../identities'
  info: Stranger <stranger@example.net>
\

The options are the same as those of \c{mailmap} (see \l{authors(1)} for
details).

\h1#listing|Listing People|

The register is the one \c{mailmap} reads and its format and rules are
described in the \c{mailmap} manual. For \c{authors} it has one more value,
\c{authors}, which specifies how the person is listed:

\dl|

\li|\c{name}

By the preferred name. This is the default and so most people don't need
the value at all.|

\li|\c{email}

By the preferred name and email, in the \c{\i{name} <\i{email}>} form.|

\li|\c{none}

Not at all. This is the value for bots, as well as for anyone who asks not
to be listed.||

Since the value is the person's choice, it is recorded once (in the
register) and every repository they contributed to lists them the same
way. Changing
it changes the entry in every \c{AUTHORS} on the next update.

The entries must tell the people listed apart. Two people with the same name
are listed with the same entry unless their emails appear, so \c{authors}
refuses to list them by name alone:

\
error: people listed in '/src/hello/AUTHORS' cannot be told apart
  info: Sam Poe
  info: list them with 'authors: email' in 'identities'
\

\h1#architecture|How authors Works|

In this chapter we look at where the people \c{authors} lists come from
and at how it writes the file.

\h#architecture-contributors|Contributors|

The contributors of a repository are the people its history identities
belong to. The history identities are the commit authors and the
\c{Co-authored-by} trailer values in the history of \c{HEAD}, taken as git
records them, the same as in \c{mailmap}. Each identity is attributed to its
person the same way too: an alias with a name takes precedence, then the
email alone, with names and emails compared case-insensitively. Since
\c{mailmap} proves that the \c{.mailmap} it generates maps every identity
to exactly this person, \c{AUTHORS} and \c{.mailmap} always attribute an
identity to the same person.

Note that it is the author of a commit that counts, so a commit applied by
someone else (for example, a rebased pull request) credits its author
only.

\h#architecture-entries|Existing Entries|

Before writing \c{AUTHORS}, \c{authors} reads the entries of the existing
file and adds the people they belong to. An entry with an email,
\c{Jane Doe <jane@example.org>} for example, is attributed like a history
identity. An entry without an email is attributed by name: it belongs to
the person whose preferred name or name alias it is, provided there is
exactly one such person. As a result, an entry that names Jane by an old
name (\c{J. Doe}, for example) still belongs to her.

Every entry is then written the way the register currently lists its
person. If Jane changes her name in the register, then her entry follows
on the next update. A person only leaves \c{AUTHORS} if the register says
\c{authors: none} for them.

An entry that the register does not attribute is an error, the same as an
unattributed history identity. If an entry without an email could be more
than one person, then this is an error as well and \c{authors} lists the
people it matches (their entries need their emails to tell them apart).

\h#architecture-file|The File|

\c{AUTHORS} starts with a fixed preamble, followed by an empty line and the
entries, one per line. The entries are unique and sorted case-insensitively
in the bytewise order, which is the same on every machine, whatever its
locale. As a result, running \c{authors} again without changes to the
history or the register produces the same file, byte for byte, and an
\c{AUTHORS} that is up to date is left alone.

The preamble is also how \c{authors} recognizes the file as its own. If an
\c{AUTHORS} does not start with the preamble, then there is no telling
where its entries are and \c{authors} leaves the file alone, asking for the
entries to be put after the preamble:

\
error: '/src/hello/AUTHORS' does not start with the AUTHORS preamble
  info: put the entries to keep after the preamble or remove the file
\

Installing \c{AUTHORS} replaces the file, which would turn a symbolic link
into a copy of its target. As a result, an \c{AUTHORS} that is a symbolic
link (for example, in a package directory, pointing to the \c{AUTHORS} of
the repository) is refused and it is the file it refers to that should be
updated.

\h1#restart|Restarted Repositories|

When a repository is restarted, its new history begins with the first
commit of the new repository and does not carry the people who contributed
to the old one. Their contributions are still there, in the files that were
imported, and so the new \c{AUTHORS} needs to list them as well.

To do that, we seed the new \c{AUTHORS} with the people of the old
repository. The simplest way is to start from the old repository's
\c{AUTHORS}, if it was generated by \c{authors}, since its entries are
already in the right form. Otherwise, we write the preamble followed by one
entry per contributor, for example, taken from \c{git shortlog} of the
imported files:

\
$ git -C ../old shortlog -sne HEAD -- src/ | cut -f 2
Jane Doe <jane@example.org>
John Roe <john@example.org>
\

We then run \c{authors} as usual. The seeded entries are attributed
through the register and written the way it lists their people, and the
contributors of the new history are added to them. From then on the seeded
people stay listed on every update, since \c{AUTHORS} only grows.
"
